<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. A typical cold after a known contact, with a low-grade temperature, a clear chest, and a heart rate under 100. Antibiotics are not indicated. The obvious viral source, the absence of night sweats and focal bone or joint pain, and a normal abdominal and spinal examination make a brucellosis relapse improbable. [S]<list><item>Supportive care: fluids, honey for the cough, saline nasal rinse, and rest.</item><item>Acetaminophen 1 g up to every 6 hours as needed, no more than 3 g a day.</item><item>Repeat a home COVID-19 antigen test if symptoms change.</item><item>Following the infectious diseases plan, call if a temperature of 38 °C or more lasts longer than three days, or if night sweats or new back or joint pain appear; blood cultures would then be drawn.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">ME/CFS flare triggered by infection (G93.32)</content>. A flare, a worsening beyond her usual day-to-day variation that is expected to last days, not a relapse. Orthostatic symptoms are worse with the infection and lower fluid intake. [S]<list><item>Continue the written flare plan: reduce activity well below her usual baseline, rest in short frequent periods, and postpone non-essential work.</item><item>Keep fluids at 2 to 2.5 L a day with extra salt; sit or lie down for tasks.</item><item>As symptoms settle, return to her pre-illness activity level in small, flexible steps guided by her diary, not by fixed increases.</item><item>Cough and fatigue may take one to two weeks to settle.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Myalgic encephalomyelitis (or encephalopathy)/chronic fatigue syndrome: diagnosis and management (NICE guideline NG206) (2021); Clinical Overview of Brucellosis (2024); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in three days. Call the same day for breathlessness, chest pain, a temperature of 38 °C or more beyond three days, night sweats, or new back or joint pain. ME/CFS review as planned in September. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-14: six-month ME/CFS care plan review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
